(a) A "nuisance" is anything that works hurt, inconvenience or damage to another, and the fact that the act done may otherwise be lawful shall not keep it from being a nuisance. The inconvenience complained of shall not be fanciful, or such as would affect only one of fastidious taste, but it shall be such as would affect an ordinarily reasonable person.
(b) The following conditions, among others, may be declared to be nuisances:
(1) Stagnant water on premises;
(2) Any dead or decaying matter, weeds, brush, vegetation, or any fruit, vegetable, animal or rodent, or any other objectionable, unsightly, or unsanitary matter upon a premises which is odorous or capable of causing disease or annoyance to the inhabitants of the city. For the purpose of this article, "weeds" means any brush, grass, vegetation, or any plant that is not regularly cultivated, that exceeds a height of ten inches; provided, however, that weeds shall not include plants in a maintained wildflower garden. Any plant that is not regularly cultivated exceeding ten inches in height shall be presumed to be a weed. "Regularly cultivated" means any plant which is routinely cared for by any person to foster its growth, including ornamental grasses, bushes, and trees;
(3) The pollution of public water or the injection of matter into the sewage system which would be damaging thereto;
(4) Maintaining a dangerous or diseased animal or fowl;
(5) Obstruction of a public street, highway or sidewalk without a permit;
(6) Loud or unnecessary noises as defined in section 34-121 et seq.;
(7) All walls, trees and buildings that may endanger persons or property;
(8) Any unsafe or unhealthful premises as defined in section 16-72 et seq.;
(9) Unused or inoperable appliances of any size or type, including, but not limited to, iceboxes, refrigerators, washers, dryers, water heaters, televisions, stereos, or any parts thereof;
(10) Any trees, shrubbery or other plants or parts thereof, which obstruct clear, safe vision on roadways and intersections of the city;
(11) Junked or abandoned motor vehicles as defined in section 34-150 et seq.;
(12) The generation of smoke or fumes in sufficient amount to cause odor or annoyance to the inhabitants of the city;
(13) Toilets or sanitary facilities, plumbing, or sewers in bad repair; and
(14) Violation of the Jonesboro Social Hosting Ordinance, as set forth in section 6-211 et seq.
(15) Any other condition constituting a nuisance under state law.
